  <h1>Quantifiers <em>much, many, a lot of</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Package a quantity through the noun you choose: <span class="ex">How much water do we need? How many bottles do we need? We need a lot of water, but we don’t need many bottles.</span></p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">50–60 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">count nouns · noncount nouns · units</span>
    <span class="tag">much · many · a lot of</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Compare one supply through an amount noun and a countable unit.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Choose the noun meaning before quantity and register.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Control questions, short answers, small amounts, excess, and agreement.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Give a fictional event inventory and solve a supply problem.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can ask about an amount or number, switch between a substance and its countable unit, describe large, small, zero, and excessive quantities, choose a natural spoken or written form, and make the verb agree with the noun.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved some in a positive offer, any in a neutral question, and plural agreement with there are.">
    <h3>Three checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the stated sentence purpose and the noun’s number.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier quantity choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="some">some</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="any">any</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="There are">There are</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="There is">There is</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a chair">a chair</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="chairs">chairs</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The coordinator offers a positive quantity for the table: We have <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="some" data-hint="Some is natural in this positive statement offering an available quantity." data-fix="We have some paper for the table." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> paper for the table.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The inventory question does not assume the answer: Do we have <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="any" data-hint="Any is natural in this neutral yes-or-no question." data-fix="Do we have any extra badges?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> extra badges?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The phrase <span class="ex">eighty chairs</span> is plural: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="There are" data-hint="A plural count noun takes there are." data-fix="There are eighty chairs in the hall." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> eighty chairs in the hall.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice one supply packaged two ways</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Riverside workshop inventory</h3>
    <p><b>Amount view:</b> <span class="ex">How much water do we have? We have a lot of water, about twenty-four liters.</span></p>
    <p><b>Unit view:</b> <span class="ex">How many bottles of water do we have? We have forty-eight bottles.</span></p>
    <p><b>Other supplies:</b> <span class="ex">We have a lot of chairs. We don’t have many printed maps. We have only a little time before the doors open.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Does the real-world supply change between <span class="ex">water</span> and <span class="ex">bottles of water</span>?</li>
      <li>Which noun asks for an amount? Which noun asks for a number of units?</li>
      <li>Which expressions describe a large quantity, a small number, and a small amount?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You identified the count noun, noncount noun, countable unit, large-quantity phrase, nounless short answer, and written many pattern.">
    <h3>Discovery check: identify what controls the choice</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Countability and quantity choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="plural count noun">plural count noun</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="noncount noun">noncount noun</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="countable unit">countable unit</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="works with both noun types">works with both noun types</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="drops of because no noun follows">drops of because no noun follows</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a natural positive written pattern">a natural positive written pattern</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>In <span class="ex">How many chairs...?</span>, <span class="ex">chairs</span> is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="plural count noun" data-hint="You can count one chair, two chairs, and three chairs." data-fix="Chairs is a plural count noun." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>In <span class="ex">How much water...?</span>, <span class="ex">water</span> names the substance as a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="noncount noun" data-hint="Water is being measured as an undivided amount, not counted as separate items." data-fix="Water is a noncount noun in How much water...?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>In <span class="ex">How many bottles of water...?</span>, <span class="ex">bottles</span> provides a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="countable unit" data-hint="The bottles divide the water into separate units that can be counted." data-fix="Bottles provides a countable unit for the water." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">A lot of chairs</span> and <span class="ex">a lot of water</span> show that <span class="ex">a lot of</span> <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="works with both noun types" data-hint="A lot of can come before plural count nouns and noncount nouns." data-fix="A lot of works with both noun types." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>After <span class="ex">How many maps do we have?</span>, the short answer <span class="ex">A lot</span> <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="drops of because no noun follows" data-hint="Of belongs before a following noun; the nounless short answer is a lot." data-fix="A lot drops of because no noun follows." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>A report says <span class="ex">Many participants used the digital map</span>. This is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a natural positive written pattern" data-hint="Many is not restricted to questions and negatives; it is common in positive written summaries." data-fix="Many participants used the digital map is a natural positive written pattern." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: grammar packages the quantity</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Countability is not simply a physical property of an object. It belongs to the noun meaning the speaker chooses. A count noun packages separate units. A noncount noun packages a substance, activity, or idea as an amount.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>package</th><th>useful question</th><th>examples</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>separate countable units</td><td><span class="ex">How many...?</span></td><td><span class="ex">chairs, guests, bottles, pieces, cups</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>an undivided amount</td><td><span class="ex">How much...?</span></td><td><span class="ex">water, time, information, equipment, advice</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>A unit phrase changes the grammatical package: <span class="ex">much advice</span> but <span class="ex">many pieces of advice</span>; <span class="ex">much furniture</span> but <span class="ex">many pieces of furniture</span>; <span class="ex">much water</span> but <span class="ex">many bottles of water</span>. The first noun after the quantifier controls <span class="ex">much</span> or <span class="ex">many</span>.</p>
  <p class="note-line">Do not add a regular plural ending to the general noncount meanings in this lesson. Use <span class="ex">information</span>, <span class="ex">advice</span>, <span class="ex">equipment</span>, and <span class="ex">furniture</span>, not <span class="ex">informations</span>, <span class="ex">advices</span>, <span class="ex">equipments</span>, or <span class="ex">furnitures</span>. Count units when needed: <span class="ex">two pieces of information</span>, <span class="ex">three items of equipment</span>.</p>

  <h3>One noun can change its package and meaning</h3>
  <p><span class="ex">Coffee</span> is noncount when it means the drink as a substance: <span class="ex">We don’t have much coffee.</span> In a café order, <span class="ex">two coffees</span> means two servings or cups. <span class="ex">Paper</span> can mean material, while <span class="ex">two papers</span> can mean two documents or articles. The plural is possible when the meaning becomes countable.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You followed the chosen noun meaning through people, substances, units, advice, pieces, and servings.">
    <h3>Drill 1: follow the noun, not the real-world object</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Noun-package choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="many guests">many guests</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="much water">much water</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="many bottles">many bottles</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="much advice">much advice</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="many pieces of advice">many pieces of advice</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="two coffees">two coffees</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The registration list counts separate people: How <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="many guests" data-hint="Guests is a plural count noun for separate people." data-fix="How many guests are registered?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> are registered?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The coordinator asks for the total amount before choosing containers: How <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="much water" data-hint="Water names the substance as an amount." data-fix="How much water do we need?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> do we need?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The coordinator now counts the separate containers: How <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="many bottles" data-hint="Bottles is the plural countable unit." data-fix="How many bottles do we need?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> do we need?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The trainer asks about the general amount, without dividing it into suggestions: How <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="much advice" data-hint="Advice is noncount when presented as a general amount." data-fix="How much advice should the guide include?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> should the guide include?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The editor counts three separate suggestions: How <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="many pieces of advice" data-hint="Pieces creates separate countable units of advice." data-fix="How many pieces of advice are in the guide?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> are in the guide?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>A volunteer orders two separate servings: At the café counter, <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="two coffees" data-hint="In a café order, coffees can mean separate servings." data-fix="Two coffees are waiting at the café counter." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> are waiting.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose quantity, sentence environment, and register</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>form</th><th>noun type</th><th>common use</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>many</b></td><td>plural count nouns</td><td><span class="ex">How many chairs? We don’t have many. Many guests arrived.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>much</b></td><td>noncount nouns</td><td><span class="ex">How much time? We don’t have much. Too much time.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>a lot of / lots of</b></td><td>both noun types</td><td><span class="ex">a lot of chairs, lots of time</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>In everyday positive U.S. conversation, <span class="ex">a lot of</span> or informal <span class="ex">lots of</span> is usually more natural than plain positive <span class="ex">much</span>: <span class="ex">We have a lot of time</span>, not usually <span class="ex">We have much time</span>. Positive <span class="ex">many</span> is normal, especially in writing and more formal speech: <span class="ex">Many participants completed the survey.</span></p>
  <p class="note-line">These are tendencies, not sentence-type laws. <span class="ex">A lot of</span> also appears in questions and negatives: <span class="ex">Do we have a lot of time? We don’t have a lot of time.</span> <span class="ex">Much</span> is natural in positive phrases with words such as <span class="ex">too, so</span>, and <span class="ex">as</span>: <span class="ex">too much noise, so much work, as much time as we need</span>.</p>

  <h3><em>A lot of</em> before a noun; <em>a lot</em> without one</h3>
  <p>Use <span class="ex">of</span> when a noun follows: <span class="ex">We need a lot of chairs.</span> Drop <span class="ex">of</span> when the noun is understood: <span class="ex">How many chairs do we need? A lot.</span></p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected question forms, conversational and written positives, a flexible a-lot-of question, and a nounless short answer.">
    <h3>Drill 2: choose the requested environment and register</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Quantity and register choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="many">many</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="much">much</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a lot of">a lot of</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Many">Many</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a lot">a lot</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="lots">lots</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Ask for the exact number on the registration list: How <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="many" data-hint="Guests is countable, and How many asks for a number." data-fix="How many guests are registered?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> guests are registered?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Ask for the amount of setup time: How <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="much" data-hint="Time is noncount in this amount question." data-fix="How much setup time do we have?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> setup time do we have?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Use the neutral everyday positive form for a large chair quantity: We have <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a lot of" data-hint="A lot of is natural before a plural count noun in everyday positive speech." data-fix="We have a lot of chairs." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> chairs.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Begin a written summary with the requested positive count form: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Many" data-hint="Positive many is natural in this written summary." data-fix="Many participants used the digital map." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> participants used the digital map.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The coordinator expects that the answer may be a large amount and asks: Do we have <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a lot of" data-hint="A lot of is possible in a question and fits the expected large-amount contrast." data-fix="Do we have a lot of time, or only ten minutes?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> time, or only ten minutes?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The noun <span class="ex">chairs</span> is already understood: “How many do we need?” “<button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a lot" data-hint="Without a following noun, use a lot without of." data-fix="How many chairs do we need? A lot." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Place the quantity on a useful scale</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>meaning</th><th>count nouns</th><th>noncount nouns</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>zero</td><td><span class="ex">no chairs / not any chairs</span></td><td><span class="ex">no water / not any water</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>small but more than zero</td><td><span class="ex">a few chairs</span></td><td><span class="ex">a little water</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>smaller than needed or expected</td><td><span class="ex">few chairs / not many chairs</span></td><td><span class="ex">little water / not much water</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>large</td><td><span class="ex">many chairs / a lot of chairs</span></td><td><span class="ex">a lot of water</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>more than wanted or acceptable</td><td><span class="ex">too many chairs</span></td><td><span class="ex">too much water</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">A few</span> and <span class="ex">a little</span> mean “some, more than zero” and can sound positive: <span class="ex">We have a few extra chairs, so everyone can sit.</span> Bare <span class="ex">few</span> and <span class="ex">little</span> emphasize a shortage and can sound formal. In everyday U.S. conversation, <span class="ex">not many</span> and <span class="ex">not much</span> are often more natural: <span class="ex">We don’t have many chairs. We don’t have much time.</span></p>
  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">Too many</span> and <span class="ex">too much</span> do not simply mean “a large quantity.” They mean the quantity passes a limit for the situation. Seventy chairs can be a lot but not too many in a hall for eighty people.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished useful small quantities, shortages, and excessive countable and noncount quantities.">
    <h3>Drill 3: use the consequence to choose the quantity</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Quantity-scale choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a few">a few</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a little">a little</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="few">few</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="little">little</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="too many">too many</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="too much">too much</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Four extra chairs are enough for the four late guests: We have <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a few" data-hint="A few means a small count above zero, and the context says the chairs are enough." data-fix="We have a few extra chairs, so all four late guests can sit." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> extra chairs.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Ten minutes is a small amount, but it is enough for the short check: We have <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a little" data-hint="A little means a small noncount amount above zero." data-fix="We have a little time, enough for the short check." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> time.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Only two maps remain for seventy-two guests, so the coordinator emphasizes the shortage: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="few" data-hint="Bare few emphasizes that the small count is insufficient." data-fix="Few printed maps remain, so display the digital map." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> printed maps remain.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The doors open in one minute, so a formal announcement emphasizes the shortage: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="little" data-hint="Bare little emphasizes an insufficient noncount amount and suits the formal announcement." data-fix="Little setup time remains, so stop adding decorations." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> setup time remains.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The aisle holds ten tables safely, but the plan places twelve there: There are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="too many" data-hint="Tables is countable, and twelve passes the stated safe limit of ten." data-fix="There are too many tables in the aisle." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> tables in the aisle.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The container holds twenty liters, but the order sends twenty-four: There is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="too much" data-hint="Water is noncount, and twenty-four liters passes the twenty-liter limit." data-fix="There is too much water for the container." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> water for the container.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Let the noun control agreement</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>The quantifier does not decide whether the verb is singular or plural. The noun does: <span class="ex">A lot of chairs are ready</span>, but <span class="ex">A lot of equipment is ready</span>. The same contrast appears in questions: <span class="ex">How many bottles are left?</span> and <span class="ex">How much water is left?</span></p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>question</th><th>natural short answers</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">How many maps are left?</span></td><td><span class="ex">A lot. / Not many. / A few. / None.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">How much time is left?</span></td><td><span class="ex">A lot. / Not much. / A little. / None.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Sound natural: link the frame, stress the quantity or noun</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">How many</span> forms one question frame before a plural noun: <span class="ex">How many <strong>GUESTS</strong>?</span> <span class="ex">How much</span> links before a noncount noun: <span class="ex">How much <strong>TIME</strong>?</span> In neutral questions, the noun often carries the most useful stress. Stress <span class="ex">many</span> or <span class="ex">much</span> when correcting the size or type of quantity.</p>
  <p>In connected U.S. speech, <span class="ex">a lot of</span> often sounds like /ə ˈlɑɾəv/, with a quick flap in <span class="ex">lot</span> and weak <span class="ex">of</span>. Informal <span class="ex">lots of</span> links as /ˈlɑtsəv/. Keep <span class="ex">of</span> before a noun even when it is weak: <span class="ex">a lot of chairs</span>. Drop it only when the noun is absent: <span class="ex">We need a lot.</span></p>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read count question, amount question, large quantity, useful small count, shortage, and countable-serving listening check">
    <h2>Listen: what quantity package do you hear?</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your teacher reads each fictional inventory line once naturally and once more slowly. Listen for the noun, unit, quantity, and consequence. Open the script only after checking.</p>
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You heard a count question, amount question, linked large quantity, useful small count, shortage, and countable serving meaning.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening check</h3>
      <div class="quiz-list">
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="the number of chairs" data-hint="How many plus plural chairs asks for a number of separate units." data-fix="How many chairs are ready? asks for the number of chairs."><p class="quiz-question">1. What information does Clip 1 request?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the number of chairs">the number of chairs</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the weight of every chair">the weight of every chair</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the amount of floor space">the amount of floor space</button></div></div>
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="the total amount of water" data-hint="How much plus noncount water asks for the total amount." data-fix="How much water do we have? asks for the total amount of water."><p class="quiz-question">2. What does Clip 2 ask about?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the total amount of water">the total amount of water</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the number of empty bottles">the number of empty bottles</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="the price of one map">the price of one map</button></div></div>
